ting. By systematically employing the warped $μ$-bubble method, we show classification theorems for stable weighted minimal hypersurfaces in 3-manifolds with nonnegative spectral scalar curvature, and we establish band width estimates for both spectral Ricci and spectral scalar curvatures. Furthermore, we prove some splitting theorems under spectral curvature conditions, including a spectral version of the Geroch conjecture for manifolds with arbitrary ends and a result related to the Milnor conjecture.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSymmetry, Integrability and Geometry: Methods and ApplicationsSIGMA 20 (2024), 038, 25 pagesProduct Inequalities for T^×-Stabilized Scalar (https://www.imath.kiev.ua/~sigma/2024/038/sigma24-038.pdf)\nciteturn3search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Gromov14the arguments in [17, §§5.5–5.7] and [6]. ... has no local scalar flat factors.This follows by combining the ×-stabilized rigidity arguments in [12] and [29] with those in[17, §5.7] but to be honest, I did not check this in full generality.4 Sc<sup>↘</sup>↓-product inequalities, conjectures and problems4. ... Thus, in particular, the ☐∃(n,m,N)-inequality from [18] and/or equivariant separation theorem for stable μ-bubbles<sup>24</sup> along with the equalitySc<sup>×</sup>[a,b] = 4 λ<sub>1</sub>[a,b] = \\frac{4\\pi^2}{(b-a)^2}.imply the following.Proposition.\nM. Gromov14the arguments in [17, §§5.5–5.7] and [6]. In fact, the ⋊-extremality for warped manifolds is more common then non-stabilized extremality.For instance, geodesic balls in spheres and in \\(\\mathbb{R}^n\\) are not non-stably extremal: one can\nincrease their metrics without diminishing the scalar curvatures. But, probably, they are \\(\\times\\!\\downarrow\\)-extremal.3.C. Questions.(i) Which convex subsets in \\(\\mathbb{R}^n\\) are \\(\\times\\!\\downarrow\\)-extremal?  \n(ii) Which surfaces are \\(\\times\\!\\downarrow\\)-extremal?3.D. About rigidity. The p